  "query_preview": "..High pressure over the Four-Corners region will\ncontinue to support a monsoon surge into Utah over the next 5-7\n(or more) days. Evening analysis indicates that a potential MCV is\ndeveloping over central Arizona. This may rotating around the\nridge into Utah Sunday...potentially increasing the threat of\nflash flooding. This will be something to watch Sunday.\n\nLooking at CAMS this evening, fairly widespread convection is\nforecast across much of the region. HREF suggest peak rainfall\nrates Sunday will be between 0.25 to 0.50 inches per hour,\nhowever, looking at ground truth today, stronger storms were\ncapable of rates a bit higher than 0.50 inches per hour given slow\nstorm motions.\n\nAdjusted heat headlines based on latest trends. With lows \nstruggling to fall below 80 degrees for the lower deserts of \nsouthern Utah, extended the Excessive Heat Warnings for lower \nWashington County, Zion National Park and Glen Canyon National \nRecreation Area. Even though highs will be slightly cooler Sunday,\nthe lack of any meaningful relief overnight will continue to \ncreate cumulative heat stress...especially for those without \nadequate cooling and/or hydration.\n\nWith cooler temperatures forecast across central Utah, cancelled\ngoing heat products for these areas.\n\nExpect convection to continue each afternoon and evening with an\nattendant flash flooding threat through at least the next 5 to 7\ndays.",
